the first thing that immediately stood  out to me about wildstar was its  aesthetic at times it's an absolutely  gorgeous game the colors are bright and  inviting all sorts of hand-drawn  frippery brings an ornate joy to each  area herds of ghazal light grazers  elegantly leap across the screen with  such a rich looking world I'm drawn to  explore it because I might find  something unexpected if you look at it  see bright colors and exaggerated shapes  and immediately compare it to world of  warcraft you're missing out on the  bigger picture in actuality wildstar's  art design is not a whole lot like Laos  but something very much its own the  second thing that stood out to me about  wildstar is that that same art style  that makes it attractive at certain  times makes it look cluttered at other  times everything draws your eye to it  the swirling grass textures the flowers  the hanging lamps the smoking fires the  bubbling lava the dust motes and the  twinkles and the dream catchers and all  the colors of the rainbow one moment  wildstar is overwhelmingly attractive  the next it's just overwhelming sadly  III is almost here and is a bit of an  obstacle to the wildstar review process  but in advance of the review I wanted to  share a few impressions of my time with  this massively multiplayer role-playing  game and honestly most of that time has  been quite positive wildstar is not  breaking new ground its fundamentals are  firmly entrenched in the formula we've  come to expect in which you take quests  go and kill monsters team up with others  and go looking for treasure out in a big  unknown world but wildstar delivers the  expected with huge doses of character  and glee the brief character quips are  charmingly delivered the quest givers  aren't averse to cracking a few jokes  and some of the adventuring  circumstances are simply adorable the  game's story is quite typical leading  you through the details of the fight  between two factions the Exiles and the  Dominion but I admit I'm invested in  part because I witnessed the Dominion  bomb a giant tree called the elder root  the elder root was kindly when we talked  in his biomechanical design made him  stand out from all those other  giant world trees that show up in games  I cared more about that darn tree than I  ever cared about the Elder Scrolls  online blands talkative characters I'm  ready for vengeance there are eight  races to choose from in six total  classes I'm a spell slinger which is a  bit of a misleading name since pistols  are my primary weapons the extensive  skill trees make me equally adept at  being a damaged heavy class or a healer  and while I usually stick to damage I've  found some fun and healing my teammates  in the insanely chaotic player versus  player matches all of my attacks are  cone based so I never need to  specifically target enemies whatever  lands in my damaged area is unlucky  enough to get hit this system keeps me  mobile since enemies also signal their  most powerful attacks in a similar  manner but that's particularly true in  the two competitive arenas I've unlocked  so far which keep me constantly on the  go so that I can avoid incoming attacks  while staying in range of my teams  healers it's terrific fun though I admit  that the busy visuals can make it tough  to stay in control look out for review  in a few weeks time but if you're on the  fence so far and are curious about what  I think I must say wildstar isn't  overflowing with brand new ideas but  it's fun to play gives me a lot to look  at gives me a lot of ways to cooperate  and compete with others and aside from  the long queue times I sometimes get  stuck dealing with when I first log in  it's mostly stable and feels feature  complete wildstar's a game I could see  myself sticking with at the end game  content is compelling enough if only  because I like uncovering new sights  sounds and adventures  welcome  you
 

